190ddeb7-0f19-44a6-8731-588ee6dde4cb	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/press-statement-20th-meeting-european-board-digital-services	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/press-statement-20th-meeting-european-board-digital-services	Press statement of the 20th meeting of the European Board for Digital Services	On 24 September 2026, the European Board for Digital Services (“the Board”) convened in Brussels for its 20th meeting. The Board discussed, amongst other things, the Commission’s recent announcements to strengthen child safety online, the role of the Digital Services Act (DSA) in strengthening election integrity in Europe and the importance of trusted flaggers. The protection of young Europeans online has been a key priority for the Board since its creation. It welcomes the attention dedicated to strengthening the protection of minors in the digital environment in the proposal for an EU KIDS Act, presented by the European Commission on 17 September 2026. Below, you can download the full press statement of the Board. Find more information about the European Board for Digital Services . Downloads Press statement of the Board - 20th meeting Download Related topics Online platforms and e-commerce DSA - Digital Services Act Last update 24 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-24 00:00:00+00	\N	df85056ca52a465c18517b5281ccf006c8c3269a2b157760700c0870755f9eb7	2026-09-24 16:25:00.619801+00
b769ebbc-dfd3-453a-826f-c537752d047f	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/adacities-call-expression-interest-deadline-extended	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/adacities-call-expression-interest-deadline-extended	ADACities Call for Expression of Interest: deadline extended	On 15 September, the European Commission held an online Information Session on the ADACities Call for Expression of Interest (CEoI). It gave stakeholders the chance to ask questions and receive clarifications on the initiative and the call. © Getty Images metamorworks The session drew 84 participants from across the autonomous-mobility ecosystem: public authorities, automotive manufacturers, suppliers and technology companies, mobility operators, autonomous-driving and AI start-ups, research networks and associations, consultancies and infrastructure and testing organisations. Main discussion points included the role of cities and other public authorities, EU-centricity, the scope of eligible vehicles and use cases, financing, and links with the Horizon Europe bridging action and cross-border testbeds. The discussion showed a clear demand for further clarification and for practical support in building consortia. To help stakeholders find partners, the Commission will launch an EU Survey [ link will be available here on 29 September ]. Individual organisations and those not yet part of a consortium, can use it to present their ideas, expertise, capabilities, location and potential contribution and to say what kind of partners or consortium opportunities they are looking for. Further matchmaking and consortium-building support, including workshops, will be provided through the ADACities Secretariat. Confidentiality and data-protection requirements will be respected throughout. Building ADACities together The Commission reiterated that ADACities is meant to be built together with its constituency. The CEoI both identifies mature deployment ambitions and lets stakeholders flag the barriers and needs the initiative should address as it develops. Extended deadline and how to submit The deadline for submitting expressions of interest under the ADACities Call for Expression of Interest is now 20 October 2026, close of business (CET) . Stakeholders should read the ADACities Scoping Paper together with the CEoI when preparing their submissions. All expressions of interest must be sent exclusively to EU-ADACITIES@ec.europa.eu . The matchmaking survey is not a submission channel. Related topics Mobility Research and Innovation Smart and Sustainable Communities Internet of Things Artificial intelligence Related content News article 23 June 2026 Autonomous Drive Ambition Cities (ADACities) Initiative to bring autonomous driving technology to cities across the EU The European Commission has launched the Autonomous Drive Ambition Cities (ADACities) initiative to support the deployment of autonomous driving in cities across the EU. Last update 24 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-24 00:00:00+00	\N	b46d4546bc29f62eb7625483af2e4ec0ccab2cde6e49c343f679ec4bae818403	2026-09-24 16:25:00.619801+00
0241cfe7-7288-427c-823f-39ecded35266	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/platform-signatories-publish-latest-reports-under-code-conduct-disinformation	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/platform-signatories-publish-latest-reports-under-code-conduct-disinformation	Platform signatories publish latest reports under the Code of Conduct on Disinformation	Signatories of the Code of Conduct on Disinformation have published their second batch of reports since the Code’s recognition as a Digital Services Act (DSA) code of conduct. GettyImages ©SmileStudioAP Signatories of the voluntary Code of Conduct on Disinformation have published their latest reports detailing the actions they are taking under the Code to reduce the spread of disinformation online. The reports , available in the Transparency Centre of the Code , come from providers of online platforms, including Google, Meta, Microsoft and TikTok. This round of reports, covering the period from 1 January - 30 June 2026 , includes dedicated chapters on actions related to ongoing crises, notably the war in Ukraine, as well as measures to safeguard the integrity of elections. They also provide data on the implementation of platforms’ disinformation-related measures and highlight developments in their policies, tools, and partnerships. This is the second time that platform signatories have reported on the implementation of their commitments since the recognition of the Code of Practice on Disinformation as a code of conduct under the Digital Services Act (DSA) , which took effect on 1 July 2025 following the request of the signatories. Background On 13 February 2025, at the request of the signatories, the Commission and the European Board for Digital Services endorsed the Code of Practice on Disinformation as a code of conduct in the sense of Article 45 DSA. On 1 July 2025, the Code was integrated into the co-regulatory framework of the Digital Services Act. This means that signatories’ adherence to its commitments are subject to independent annual auditing, and that the Code serves as a relevant benchmark for determining compliance with the DSA. With this recognition, the Code became a significant and meaningful benchmark of DSA compliance for providers of Very Large Online Platforms and Very Large Online Search Engines (VLOPs and VLOSEs) – those used by 45 million or more monthly users in the EU - that adhere to and comply with its commitments. VLOPs and VLOSEs report every six months on the actions taken under the Code, while other non-platform signatories report annually. As indicated in the Commission’s opinion on the conversion of the Code and reaffirmed in the European Democracy Shield Communication , the Commission assesses the individual commitment levels of VLOP and VLOSE signatories and their implementation. Now that a full year of reporting on the Code’s implementation is available, the Commission will open Code dialogues with all VLOP and VLOSE signatories to discuss their subscription levels and engagement under the Code. Related topics Media and democracy Disinformation Democracy in the digital age Last update 24 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-24 00:00:00+00	\N	1ddc18cc73a9d74731287c10d35ef2ba76db2b363ad0250730221bd61e13e13f	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
9e4a514e-1d55-4a65-b266-8f83e15193cc	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/executive-vice-president-virkkunen-visits-greenland-discuss-arctic-security-and-connectivity	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/executive-vice-president-virkkunen-visits-greenland-discuss-arctic-security-and-connectivity	Executive Vice-President Virkkunen visits Greenland to discuss Arctic security and connectivity	Executive Vice-President for Tech Sovereignty, Security and Democracy Henna Virkkunen is in Nuuk, Greenland, today and tomorrow to discuss Arctic security, critical infrastructure, connectivity and the European Union's partnership with Greenland. During her visit, Executive Vice-President Virkkunen will meet Jens-Frederik Nielsen, Premier Minister of Greenland, as a follow up to a meeting between the two last year. The trip follows the visit of the President Ursula von der Leyen in September 2026, during which the EU, Greenland and Denmark signed a Joint Declaration . It comes also as the EU prepares a new Arctic Strategy to reflect evolving priorities including security, defence, connectivity and economic security. Furthermore, it builds on already close cooperation in many important areas under the portfolio of Executive Vice-President Virkkunen, such as connectivity. Today, she will meet Mariane Paviasen Jensen, Minister for Children, Youth, Justice and Gender Equality, and Iddimanngiiu Jensen Bianco, Minister for Housing, Infrastructure, Outer Districts, and Preparedness. She will then visit the Joint Arctic Command, which is tasked with protecting the sovereignty of the Kingdom of Denmark in the Arctic region, to exchange views on Arctic security with its Head, Søren Andersen. Tomorrow, Executive Vice-President Virkkunen will meet Christian Keldsen, Chief Executive Officer of Business Greenland, before meeting Jens-Frederik Nielsen, Premier Minister of Greenland. She will conclude the visit with a tour of the Tusass satellite ground station alongside its Chief Executive Officer, Jonas Hasselriis. Greenland is an integral part of the EU's Arctic policy . Our partnership supports Greenland in the pursuit of its own objectives in terms of growth, security and connectivity. The Commission has proposed increasing EU financial support from €225 million in 2021–2027 to €530 million in 2028–2034. Related topics Digital connectivity Strengthening trust and security International relations Last update 24 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-24 00:00:00+00	\N	5697593ba8e9f41e50ce64c660ca3219d73ce123a191777a715fdfd25f2f9251	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
