bdf4f4b9-7bb5-473c-b996-7288941d876c	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-and-eib-group-launch-european-institutional-investors-pact-boost-investment-european	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-and-eib-group-launch-european-institutional-investors-pact-boost-investment-european	Commission and EIB Group launch European Institutional Investors Pact to boost investment in European tech scale-ups	News article 22 September 2026 Commission and EIB Group launch European Institutional Investors Pact to boost investment in European tech scale-ups The Pact is a new voluntary framework designed to channel more long-term institutional capital into Europe's technology and scale-up ecosystem. Thirteen institutional investors have already expressed their intention to channel investments into Europe's innovation and scale-up ecosystem, including through the €15 billion European Tech Champions Initiative (ETCI) 2.0 and the €5 billion Scaleup Europe Fund.	2026-09-22 00:00:00+00	\N	c74a5c3d9e2c9d4dabb28f768c82d8876c2bc9f1abc3eb99a6cbbca20a6cfd8e	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
f1217e86-48ce-4325-9397-1c1d85e85359	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/two-commission-reports-show-impact-artificial-intelligence-and-digital-technologies-teaching-and	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/two-commission-reports-show-impact-artificial-intelligence-and-digital-technologies-teaching-and	Two Commission reports show impact of artificial intelligence and digital technologies on teaching and learning in Europe	The European Commission published two new reports exploring the implications of generative artificial intelligence (AI) for education stakeholders and practitioners, as well as looking into how European education systems are responding to the digital transformation. AdobeStock ©Drobot Dean The paper ‘Friend or foe? Evidence from the use of generative artificial intelligence in learning and teaching' highlights how generative AI (genAI) is slowly but steadily moving from a technological innovation to a mainstream tool across education and learning practices in the EU. Whether genAI is beneficial for learning outcomes ultimately depends on how the technology is used, and its overall impact on long-term skills remains unclear. Teachers therefore need substantial initial and continuous professional development to make full use of the benefits offered by genAI, supported by institutional guidelines for a responsible use of the technology. The second report ‘ Digital education at school in Europe 2026: Bridging gaps in access, teaching and learning ' shows that while digital transformation is addressed through various channels across European education systems, approaches and implementation conditions vary. Most education systems address the core components of digital infrastructure, but differences remain in school-level access, technical support, and digital environments. Digital competence is included in lower-secondary curricula across almost all systems analysed, while national or standardised assessment remains uncommon. Nearly two-thirds of education systems have developed strategies, guidance or other policy frameworks addressing AI. The findings will contribute to the Commission's work on the Union of Skills and provide evidence for the forthcoming Education Package , which will address basic skills, teachers and trainers, and digital education infrastructure. They are informed by the recent OECD Programme for International Student Assessment report. Executive Vice-President for Social Rights and Skills, Quality Jobs and Preparedness, Roxana Mînzatu , said: “Digital technologies, including Generative artificial intelligence are rapidly changing how we live, work and learn. While students and teachers stand to gain from these new possibilities, we must carefully address risks around bias, privacy, and overreliance. Digital technology should serve our citizens, and we must ensure these tools genuinely support learning.” Related topics Creating a digital society Digital learning & ICT in education Digital Skills and Jobs Artificial intelligence Last update 24 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-22 00:00:00+00	\N	105bdc6a8ece51ce0780ac5539f9db6816fea7e0aaa3e4f055ea06ee3aed0f58	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
ea04eb75-58e7-4ca6-abd5-ccfaa66360b2	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-enhances-energy-efficiency-and-sustainability-data-centres-eu	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-enhances-energy-efficiency-and-sustainability-data-centres-eu	Commission enhances energy efficiency and sustainability of data centres in the EU	News article 21 September 2026 Commission enhances energy efficiency and sustainability of data centres in the EU The European Commission proposed today a common rating scheme for data centres aimed at increasing transparency on their energy use and supporting their sustainable integration into Europe's energy system.	2026-09-21 00:00:00+00	\N	7ce21b4fe8562710219d7b91d11e6fdc2ba2e830d55e1a29f5af35ae03930cb9	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
6af87328-ed96-46b9-90f8-e23bf1c75b12	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-hosts-youth-policy-dialogue-protection-minors-online-and-cyberbullying	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-hosts-youth-policy-dialogue-protection-minors-online-and-cyberbullying	Commission hosts Youth Policy Dialogue on the protection of minors online and cyberbullying	Executive Vice-President for Tech Sovereignty, Security and Democracy, Henna Virkkunen, and Commissioner for Intergenerational Fairness, Youth, Culture and Sport, Glenn Micallef, will jointly host a Youth Policy Dialogue tomorrow in Brussels under the umbrella of the Cyberbullying Campaign organised by the European Commission and European Schoolnet. Young participants will share their experiences, concerns, and ideas on the protection of minors online and the creation of a safer digital environment and cyberbullying. They will outline the main challenges young people encounter online and talk about possible solutions. The discussion will also explore EU initiatives to address these issues, including the EU Cyberbullying Action Plan , the Digital Services Act , the Better Internet for Kids strategy , and the proposed EU KIDS Act . The Dialogue will explore how young people can feel safer and more confident online, focusing on safeguards, digital literacy, measures to prevent and address cyberbullying, and the skills needed to navigate digital spaces safely and responsibly. Executive Vice-President Virkkunen said: “Protecting children and young people in the digital world is a shared responsibility. Today's dialogue is an opportunity to listen to their voices, understand their concerns, and work together to create a safer online environment. The European Commission remains committed to ensuring that our digital spaces are secure, inclusive, and empowering for all young Europeans.” Commissioner Micallef said: “Being online is part of growing up today. Being bullied should not be. Tomorrow, we will listen directly to young people about what makes them feel unsafe online, what actually helps and what needs to change.” The session will be livestreamed online . Youth Policy Dialogues are part of the Commission's commitment to mainstream the voice of young people in EU policymaking, as set out in President von der Leyen 's Political Guidelines for 2024-2029 . Related topics Better Internet for Children Strengthening trust and security Online platforms and e-commerce DSA - Digital Services Act Last update 21 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-21 00:00:00+00	\N	0966605765ec5675e226d70b5927784bc60fd507b36cdcbd8884e4e5af69ba7f	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
