43bfe6cb-e1fb-4599-b1dd-0ec96a8cd4e4	0ea9f4cc-9c79-5e75-b93e-2d8edb170f5a	https://kiesa.rks-gov.net/Page.aspx?id=1,5,1186	https://kiesa.rks-gov.net/Page.aspx?id=1,5,1186	Nis ndërtimi i Parkut Industrial në Podujevë, investim mbi 3 milionë euro	Nis ndërtimi i Parkut Industrial në Podujevë, investim mbi 3 milionë euro Prishtinë, 17/08/2026 Kryeministri i Republikës së Kosovës, Albin Kurti, ministrja e Industrisë, Ndërmarrësisë, Tregtisë dhe Inovacionit, Mimoza Kusari-Lila, dhe kryetari i Komunës së Podujevës, Shpejtim Bulliqi, kanë vendosur gurthemelin për ndërtimin e Parkut Industrial në fshatin Livadicë të Podujevës, vetëm një muaj pas nënshkrimit të marrëveshjes së bashkëpunimit ndërmjet MINTI-t dhe Komunës së Podujevës. Parku Industrial përbën një investim të rëndësishëm për ekonominë e Llapit dhe për bizneset që tashmë kanë shprehur interes të madh për të investuar dhe zgjeruar aktivitetin e tyre në këtë park të ri. Parku Industrial do të shtrihet në një sipërfaqe prej rreth 30 hektarësh, ndërsa investimi për ndërtimin e tij kap vlerën prej mbi 3 milionë euro. Projekti pritet të përfundojë brenda 350 ditësh pune, duke krijuar një hapësirë të re dhe moderne për zhvillimin e bizneseve dhe industrisë. Parku do të ndërtohet në harmoni me standardet për mbrojtjen e mjedisit dhe parimet e tranzicionit të gjelbër. Në pesë vitet e ardhshme, vizioni i Ministrisë së Industrisë, Ndërmarrësisë, Tregtisë dhe Inovacionit është zhvillimi i 16 parqeve industriale në të gjithë Kosovën, me një investim që tejkalon 50 milionë euro. Në zhvillimin e parqeve të reja industriale po integrohen standardet e parqeve industriale të gjelbra, parimet e ekonomisë qarkore dhe koncepti i simbiozës industriale. Kjo qasje nënkupton që bizneset brenda një parku të mos funksionojnë si njësi të izoluara, por të krijojnë mundësi për bashkëpunim dhe përdorim më efikas të burimeve. Një biznes mund të përdorë mbetjet ose nënproduktet e një biznesi tjetër si lëndë të parë, ndërsa disa biznese mund të ndajnë infrastrukturën, energjinë, sistemet e trajtimit të mbetjeve apo shërbime të tjera. Në këtë kuadër, synohet që uji, energjia dhe lëndët e para të përdoren në mënyrë sa më efikase. Në këtë mënyrë, ekonomia qarkore bëhet një mekanizëm për uljen e kostove dhe rritjen e konkurrueshmërisë së bizneseve. Kjo është veçanërisht e rëndësishme për ndërmarrjet vendore, të cilat duhet të konkurrojnë jo vetëm në tregun e Kosovës, por edhe në tregjet rajonale dhe evropiane. Për këtë qëllim, Ministria e Industrisë, Ndërmarrësisë, Tregtisë dhe Inovacionit është duke avancuar bashkëpunimin me Bankën Botërore dhe IFC-në, me synim sjelljen e ekspertizës ndërkombëtare në planifikimin, zhvillimin dhe menaxhimin e parqeve industriale. Parku Industrial i Podujevës është pjesë e vizionit për zhvillimin e një rrjeti modern dhe të qëndrueshëm të parqeve industriale në komunat e Republikës së Kosovës, me synim të qartë për krijimin e më shumë mundësive për investime, rritjen e prodhimit vendor, zgjerimin e eksporteve dhe hapjen e vendeve të reja të punës. Ky projekt nuk përfaqëson vetëm një investim në infrastrukturë, por edhe një investim në një model të ri të zhvillimit ekonomik, ku industria, inovacioni dhe mbrojtja e mjedisit ecin së bashku.	2026-08-17 00:00:00+00	\N	92698e7b572e37a389243e36622e67b97c8d930e3daff80196028a50ecbfba19	2026-09-24 12:00:00.911345+00
d1109ae4-2609-4232-bc8c-8f9ac1e19f91	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/ai-board-holds-its-ninth-meeting	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/ai-board-holds-its-ninth-meeting	AI Board holds its ninth meeting	During the meeting, participants discussed the latest developments of EU and international AI policy as well as various aspects around AI Act enforcement and implementation. On 17 September 2026, the AI Board met under the chairmanship of the Irish Presidency of the EU Council to discuss and review the current priorities for implementing EU AI Policy. This AI Board meeting was the first attended by Moldova as an observer. Issues discussed Discussions focused on the current policy developments at EU and international level around AI, the Commission’s enforcement activities and priorities, updates related to the AI Act implementation, and the publication of the Commission’s Action Plan on cybersecurity and AI . The Commission provided an overview of new capabilities around frontier AI, covering, among others, recent AI incidents. Discussions also revolved around the strengthening of Europe’s own AI capabilities and frontier technologies, AI safety, AI uptake across key industries, and further coordination among the Member States. The Commission also presented an update on AI Act implementation, including on the development of recommendations to support AI literacy and measures to support the transparency rules that became applicable on 2 August this year (namely the Code of Practice and the guidelines ). Other agenda items concerned: cooperation on market surveillance and the governance structures around pre-market conformity assessment, as foreseen in the AI Act possibility to introduction of a secondment programme for market surveillance authorities to the EDPS the AI Board's governance structures The Board members were also updated on the Action Plan on Cybersecurity and AI. Highlights included the development evaluation and testing of infrastructure around cybersecurity capabilities of frontier AI and preparing the EU’s cyber ecosystem for challenges and opportunities. Next meeting The tenth AI Board meeting is expected to take place on 18th November on the sidelines of the Apply AI Summit taking place on 17th November in Brussels. This Summit will conclude the European AI Innovation Month , which will be opened on 14th October in Dublin at the International AI Summit . Related topics Artificial intelligence Last update 18 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-18 00:00:00+00	\N	19f1ee6b4cdde8c18c103bc9155e57e1e4342bdce53e649e000fb8cb7ed88a11	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
744c783b-cb02-4035-9452-6e6783aec0d3	84cfcda8-29d1-57ac-b23f-48d70e57393f	https://kiesa.rks-gov.net/Page.aspx?id=1,5,1199	https://kiesa.rks-gov.net/Page.aspx?id=1,5,1199	LISTA PRELIMINARE E PËRFITUESVE SIPAS THIRRJES THIRRJE PUBLIKE PËR PËRKRAHJEN FINANCIARE TË NMVM-VE NË SKEMËN E GRANTEVE PËR ENERGJI TË PASTËR – FAZA 2	LISTA PRELIMINARE E PËRFITUESVE SIPAS THIRRJES THIRRJE PUBLIKE PËR PËRKRAHJEN FINANCIARE TË NMVM-VE NË SKEMËN E GRANTEVE PËR ENERGJI TË PASTËR – FAZA 2 Prishtinë, 18/09/2026 LISTA PRELIMINARE E PËRFITUESVE SIPAS THIRRJES THIRRJE PUBLIKE PËR PËRKRAHJEN FINANCIARE TË NMVM-VE NË SKEMËN E GRANTEVE PËR ENERGJI TË PASTËR – FAZA 2	2026-09-18 00:00:00+00	\N	505e3bcd59308b562734fe62088fd6c66157495b005fdb99253b287a40bdaa96	2026-09-24 12:00:39.341384+00
32e43292-133f-41a7-b9d9-d5fd99569395	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-and-korea-deepen-cooperation-secure-satellite-connectivity	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-and-korea-deepen-cooperation-secure-satellite-connectivity	EU and Korea deepen cooperation on secure satellite connectivity	The European Commission and the Republic of Korea have agreed to strengthen cooperation on secure satellite connectivity and explore future integration with IRIS², the EU's secure connectivity constellation. © Andrey Suslov For the Republic of Korea, this could open access to the programme's architecture, while the EU space industry could draw on Korean industrial expertise. The administrative arrangement was signed by Andrius Kubilius, Commissioner for Defence and Space, and Giyoung Jeoung, Deputy Minister for the Advanced Capabilities Program of the Republic of Korea. The agreement sets up a working group to exchange information on existing and forthcoming secure‑connectivity programmes, explore joint applications and use cases, and assess technical solutions for cooperation at system level with IRIS² . The working group brings together the Commission, the European Space Agency , and the Republic of Korea's Defense Acquisition Program Administration and Agency for Defense Development, alongside representatives from other agencies and national authorities. The first phase of IRIS² is scheduled for launch by the end of 2029. In August 2026, the conclusion of negotiations on the programme's governance, technical set-up and financing confirmed the system architecture and shortlisted the main suppliers for the programme. IRIS² is open to cooperation with third countries through international treaties, and such treaties have so far been signed with Norway and Iceland . Related topics Broadband Digital connectivity Satellite broadband International relations Hybrid security threats Last update 18 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-17 00:00:00+00	\N	30b120b485732332f892f73cde84147a6c33a1f6f89fcd235989ba7cc933928d	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
960b88bf-f8b3-41eb-86b2-7843411544d6	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/european-citizens-panel-democratic-resilience-will-bring-eu-citizens-together-discuss-ways	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/european-citizens-panel-democratic-resilience-will-bring-eu-citizens-together-discuss-ways	European Citizens' Panel on Democratic Resilience will bring EU citizens together to discuss ways to strengthen our democracies	From 18 to 20 September, 150 EU citizens representing the EU – randomly selected from all Member States - will kick off their deliberations in the European Citizens’ Panel on Democratic Resilience, a key deliverable announced under the European Democracy Shield. ©Comeback Images The participants will discuss the challenges facing our democracies, share their experiences and ideas, and develop recommendations for future EU action, including on how to help citizens partake in democratic life and strengthen democratic resilience. Thanks to the ongoing communication campaign “Protect What Matters: Democracy”, which encourages EU citizens to share their views and aims to ensure broader participation on ongoing debates, all those interested will also have the opportunity to participate from wherever they are. EU citizens will be able to share their ideas also via an interactive online debate on the Citizens’ Engagement Platform , as well as by citizens’ panels and participatory events at local level . Henna Virkkunen, Executive Vice-President for Tech Sovereignty, Security, and Democracy, said: “Citizens are the heartbeat of European democracy, and this Panel shows the importance of involving different voices from across the EU to shape our future. In a world where democracies are under pressure, including from online threats such as disinformation, manipulation, and polarisation, people can make the difference, by speaking and staying connected with one another.” Michael McGrath , Commissioner for Democracy, Justice, the Rule of Law and Consumer Protection, said: “With this Panel, we are putting people at the heart of our work. We want to hear directly from citizens about their experiences, ideas and concerns for the health of our future democracies. This is democracy in action: giving people a real say in how we protect what matters. Together we can ensure that our democracies remain open, fair, and alive”. A second online session will take place on 16-18 October, and a final session, scheduled in Brussels on 20-22 November, will bring the deliberations to fruition. By the end of the process, the Panel will develop a set of recommendations which will feed into the Commission’s work on strengthening democracy and making it more resilient. The livestream and recordings of the discussions will be available. Journalists wishing to attend in person may contact us . Read more about the European Citizens' Panel and on the European Democracy Shield . Related topics Media and democracy Disinformation Democracy in the digital age Last update 17 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-17 00:00:00+00	\N	e35ac2d3bb7f3ee401a528af9b53172f7e0ea7c93df3a6186cc303431bdf01b7	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
7fb0aa00-2e6c-485e-a675-49a4c6ef2165	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-kids-act-restrict-social-media-platforms-access-children-eu	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-kids-act-restrict-social-media-platforms-access-children-eu	EU KIDS Act to restrict social media platforms’ access to children in the EU	The European Commission has adopted the EU KIDS Act to enhance the online safety of children throughout the Union. The proposal prohibits social media platforms from accessing children under the age of 13 and sets an EU-wide minimum age for minors to open an account of their own at the age of 15, thereby ensuring a gradual approach. The KIDS Act also reverses the burden of proof. Now, service providers will have to show that their services are age-appropriate and safe by design. Commission President, Ursula von der Leyen , said: “Today, our children are engaging with the most sophisticated technologies ever created. Technology that was never created with their wellbeing in mind. Our KIDS Act is reversing the burden of proof - it is for platforms to show they are safe by design. And we put parents back in the driving seat, giving them the tools to help their children navigate a safer online world.” Read the full press release and factsheet . Find further information: Kids Act - Proposal and supporting documents KIDS Act - questions and answers Related topics Better Internet for Children Strengthening trust and security Online platforms and e-commerce Related content Policy and legislation 17 September 2026 Proposal for EU KIDS Act - 'EU Keeping Internet Digital Spaces Accountable and Trustworthy' This proposal seeks to protect minors from risky digital services and AI systems, uphold the digital single market and maintain a coherent regulatory framework and enforcement structure for the protection of minors online. Last update 22 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-17 00:00:00+00	\N	b3056ad9da086780a97a76427971fc5618fe202fc27f20d3c7a77ace4525c75c	2026-09-24 12:01:48.963579+00
8d366ae4-bf88-46d0-8d69-9faa84ac90af	8bd56782-c68d-5f64-8cf7-a273db12bcad	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-welcomes-design-first-important-project-common-european-interest-ai	https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-welcomes-design-first-important-project-common-european-interest-ai	Commission welcomes the design of first Important Project of Common European Interest in AI	The European Commission welcomes the initiative of 19 Member States to create and pre-notify under State aid rules the first Important Project of Common European Interest (IPCEI) in the field of artificial intelligence (AI). AdobeStock © Supatman The project aims at developing a range of decentralised and commonly accessible AI services. IPCEI AI covers the entire AI stack and aims to develop advanced and highly innovative AI and compute management technologies and applications, industrial products and services going beyond the state of the art in the sector concerned. It will also aim to drive technological advances while contributing to European digital sovereignty, thereby positioning Europe as a leader in AI innovation and reinforcing Europe's AI value chain from technological development to adoption. This aims at contributing to the target of the EU becoming a global leader in AI, as set out in the Commission’s AI Continent Action Plan , the Apply AI Strategy and the proposal for a Cloud and AI Development Act . The IPCEI AI has been jointly designed by 19 Member States (Austria, Belgium, Croatia, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Hungary, Ireland, Italy, Latvia, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Poland, Romania, Slovakia, Slovenia, Spain and Sweden) and is coordinated by Germany. The Commission has facilitated this endeavour through the IPCEI Design Support Hub , established in April 2025, in which Commission services help Member States to prepare State aid notification for the projects to ensure compliance with the EU rules from the outset. This should allow for a quicker processing of the State aid notification. As part of IPCEI AI, 11 Member States involved that will grant State aid to direct participants announced that they will start pre-notifying their projects to the Commission in September 2026. The Commission will then assess the compliance of the projects with the eligibility and compatibility criteria of the IPCEI Communication . Related topics Artificial intelligence Last update 17 September 2026 Print as PDF	2026-09-16 00:00:00+00	\N	55b865e91d44959bb433a372bbe8dd1aba6efcb76645959b9adf71e8c90e241e	2026-09-24 12:15:01.57535+00
